00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:10.730
And we'll try to keep
it light, and we'll

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:13.354
try and take some questions, and
talk through some common cases

00:01:13.354 --> 00:01:14.880
as well.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.850
So, for me, as I was
thinking about how

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:18.700
we were going to
put this together,

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.380
the thing I sort of asked
myself is, where did we

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.300
learn to model data?

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.100
And some people didn't
learn to model data,

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.200
they just have an ORM,
or something modeled data

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.110
for them, or they inherited
it, and that's sometimes tough.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.729
But where did we
learn to model data?

00:01:31.729 --> 00:01:33.770
And I think for most of
us the answer is actually

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:36.940
quite simple, Codd.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:40.860
Oh, no, not that Cod, this Codd.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.490
This is Edgar Codd.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.730
If you guys haven't
seen Edgar Codd before,

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:50.950
he is due a great debt in
the data and analytics space.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.750
So Edgar Codd is the man who
created the relational data

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:54.250
model.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:57.970
And he started this with
a paper he wrote in 1970

00:01:57.970 --> 00:01:59.830
called "A Relational
Model for Data

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:02.020
in Large Shared Databanks."

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:05.330
Quick history note, back in the
'70s, we didn't have databases,

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:06.520
we had databanks.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:10.150
Same thing, but that's
what they were called.

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:13.502
Now in this paper, Codd
outlines the foundation

00:02:13.502 --> 00:02:14.710
of a relational model, right?

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:18.760
So he defines a system of
tuples, and importantly,

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.084
an algebra on those tuples.

00:02:21.084 --> 00:02:23.500
And this is the thing that
leads us to languages like SQL,

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.710
where we can have declarative
languages that are very easily

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:29.140
able to produce
execution plans that we

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:30.650
can use to analyze data.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.920
And this gets us a
long, long way, right?

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.734
Like this gets us from the very
early databases of the '70s,

00:02:36.734 --> 00:02:39.400
to all the things we can do with
big data processing and systems

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.290
like BigQuery today.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:45.370
Now, in that, Codd had two
really primary concerns.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.770
Data independence and
data inconsistency.

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:49.840
So in his mind, these
were the things that

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.070
had to be addressed, right?

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.682
And part of the value
of the relational model

00:02:53.682 --> 00:02:55.390
is that you get data
independence, right?

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:57.640
Your data looks a little
bit like relationships

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.010
in the real world, it's
easy to add fields,

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:01.610
it's easy to do stuff with it.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:04.420
But it also helps guard
against data inconsistency.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.670
And data inconsistency
can lead to wrong results,

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:10.360
and miscomputed bills,
and all kinds of pain.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:12.502
And if you guys are
familiar with Codd,

00:03:12.502 --> 00:03:14.710
you may have heard of these
things in your university

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:16.810
class, normal forms, right?

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:18.980
And this starts with
the first normal form,

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:21.280
which is introduced
in the paper in 1970.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.590
And with the help of others
evolved all the way up

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:25.629
through the fifth normal form.

00:03:25.629 --> 00:03:27.670
Now, many of us who work
in the database industry

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:29.410
may remember taking
schemas and saying

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:34.180
I've got to get to 3NF, 4NF,
or BCNF, which is Boyce Codd

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:39.880
Normal Form, or even to fifth NF
in the most extreme situations.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:42.740
Now there are lots of good
reasons for normalization,

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:43.240
right?

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:46.360
So increasing data
independence is actually great.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:50.590
Because it makes this easy for
us to extend our models of what

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:51.640
the world looks like.

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:55.124
Our data can well represent
what happens in the world.

00:03:55.124 --> 00:03:56.790
And again, reducing
data and consistency

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:00.160
is great because anomalies
are confusing and problematic.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:03.700
So what do we mean when
we say easy to extend?

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.370
Add new types, mirror
the real world,

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:07.430
I've said that a few times now.

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:10.237
Avoiding anomalies can
be different things.

00:04:10.237 --> 00:04:12.820
One of the things that Codd was
really, really concerned about

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:16.089
was avoiding duplicates
and conflicting values.

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:19.420
Conflicting values in particular
can remain a challenge,

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:21.730
but the other thing that
Codd was dealing with

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:23.740
were operational constraints.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:25.220
I mean, Codd was
a man of his time,

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.178
he was a man at the
cutting edge of technology,

00:04:27.178 --> 00:04:31.030
and he was making sure that
the system he was describing

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:34.470
fit well with the operational
constraints of the day.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:36.999
And this was the
constraint of the day.

00:04:36.999 --> 00:04:39.040
You may have never seen
a picture of this before.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:41.070
This is the IBM System/360.

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:46.015
This is the premier database
platform from 1964 to 1978.

00:04:46.015 --> 00:04:50.100
And it ran a hierarchical
database called IMS.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:54.360
IMS well, IMS is now 51
years old, and I think still

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.220
runs in production
in many places.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:00.300
But so Codd's primary argument
was hierarchical databases,

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.835
like IMF--

00:05:01.835 --> 00:05:06.900
IMS, sorry-- can have these
problems of consistency,

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:08.910
have these problems
of data duplication,

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:11.790
can be really difficult to make
model lots of general purpose

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:12.930
situations.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:16.350
And so his relational
model made it much simpler

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:18.600
for us to capture and
represent information

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.370
in a queriable fashion
that could answer

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:24.620
a broad set of constraints.

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:26.172
Things though have changed.

00:05:26.172 --> 00:05:28.130
Most of us don't come to
work every day in sort

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:30.560
of a burnt umber kind
of room, with spinning

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.200
tapes, and looking
very fashionable,

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:34.520
sometimes I wish I did.

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:37.250
But things have changed.

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:40.550
And because things have
changed, its worthwhile

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:43.940
when we think about modeling
data, to say, yes, the things

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:45.542
that Codd was after
were important,

00:05:45.542 --> 00:05:47.750
and they and they matter to
how we represent our data

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:49.237
and how we analyze it.

00:05:49.237 --> 00:05:51.320
But some things have
probably changed with respect

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:54.230
to our operational
constraints, and as such we

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:57.480
should think about maybe we
could do things differently.

00:05:57.480 --> 00:05:59.070
So again, I don't I
don't want to make

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:01.820
anyone think that
normalization is not important.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:03.870
Normalized data models
are really important,

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:05.370
they still matter.

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:07.930
Data is moving fast, it's
changing all the time,

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:10.290
and so this notion of
data independence is huge.

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:11.940
It still matters quite a lot.

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:13.890
And nobody wants wrong
results, wrong results

00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:16.380
are just unacceptable.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.810
Now access and simplicity
of queries though, I think,

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.180
matter far more
than they ever did.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.910
And part of the reason of this
is that more and more people

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:24.900
are analyzing data.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:28.060
More and more people are
analyzing lots of data.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:30.660
And so making it
simple for other people

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:33.330
to write queries against
data, or to build reports

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:36.250
against data, is really,
really important as well.

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:38.580
So the question I
keep coming back to,

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:41.340
is how do we challenge these
sort of normal assumptions

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:42.650
in modern environments?

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:44.830
What are the things
that are different?

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:47.460
One of the things I think
is really, hugely different

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:49.890
in modern distributed
data processing systems,

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:53.430
is that duplicate records don't
cost money like they used to.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:56.610
If we go back to our
picture of the IBM 360,

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:59.640
that data was stored on tapes,
or on giant giant magnetic hard

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:00.180
drives.

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:03.990
The cost of a megabyte of
storage in the '60s and '70s

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:05.040
was tremendous.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:07.620
And so the graph we have here
is basically average cost

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:10.840
of a gigabyte in a year,
starting from 1980.

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:13.410
It didn't go all the
way back to the '70s.

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:16.740
And if we see, in this, is
it's basically dropped to zero.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:18.600
So when we think
about normalization,

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:20.700
when we think about
our data modeling,

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:24.330
one of the real specters
of denormalization

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:27.090
kind of goes away, because
the price just drops out.

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:30.000
Having a duplicate
record is fine.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.990
Now if a duplicate record causes
inconsistency, that's an issue.

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:37.024
So this allows us to kind of
relax some of the constraints

00:07:37.024 --> 00:07:38.940
that we might have heard
of when we were going

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:40.350
through our university courses.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:42.780
So the old Codd's
rule, you know,

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:45.240
"the key, and nothing but the
key, so help me Codd," right?

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:48.390
We can reduce that a little bit.

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:51.150
The notion of these things
like non-prime attributes,

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:54.090
we can have more stuff in
tables than we used to,

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:56.670
because it doesn't cost us
a fortune to store data,

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:58.960
or to store small
amounts of data.

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:01.770
We also have the ability now
to think about complex types

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:03.990
stored inside rows in
a way that we couldn't.

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:05.864
And this is a thing that
BigQuery is actually

00:08:05.864 --> 00:08:09.179
quite good at it, and somewhat
natively designed for.

00:08:09.179 --> 00:08:10.970
The other thing is that
distributed systems

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:12.470
are the new normal.

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:15.620
When Codd was writing
his paper in 1970,

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:17.720
networks weren't a thing.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:21.140
They weren't a thing that
query processors had access to.

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:24.080
Nowadays, when we look at
big data systems like Hadoop.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:26.380
Spark, things like that,
and systems like BigQuery,

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:28.790
distributed systems
are the way we process

00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:30.540
massive amounts of data.

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:34.100
Now, that means that there
are new costs to consider.

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:36.200
Now, just a quick
show of hands, I

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:38.970
don't even know what a
shuffle operation is?

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:41.470
All right, so a few people know
what a shuffle operation is,

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:42.669
a few people don't.

00:08:42.669 --> 00:08:44.500
Just to make sure
people understand,

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:47.470
if I have a multistage
distributed process

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:50.650
and I have to pass data from
one set of workers to another,

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:53.380
that effectively amounts
to shuffling data around.

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:55.780
Now this is costly because
I have to take those bytes,

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:57.970
I've got to serialize, I've
got to put them on a protocol

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:00.400
stack, I got to send them down
to the nick, across the wire,

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:02.025
and bring it all the
way back up again.

00:09:02.025 --> 00:09:03.730
And that's a cost we
didn't used to have

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:06.130
in single monolithic systems.

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:09.430
So this means when we do things
like joining data, there a cost

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:11.230
because there's transport
that's happening.

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:13.780
And so, while joins
are incredibly useful

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:16.810
and they're a key part of
the way anyone analyzes data,

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:19.780
we do have to ask ourselves,
is a join really improving

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:22.890
my workload, or is this
join just something

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:25.090
I've incurred
because I've blindly

00:09:25.090 --> 00:09:29.510
normalized data according
to rules from the past.

00:09:29.510 --> 00:09:31.550
So the other thing is
that columnar access

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:34.010
has become a big deal for
analytical processing.

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:36.990
So back in the '70s
and then onward,

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:38.570
we talk about OLTP systems.

00:09:38.570 --> 00:09:40.760
These are usually row
oriented, and this

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:42.980
means that when we
access data, we're

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:44.824
accessing in a row at a time.

00:09:44.824 --> 00:09:46.490
And this is one of
the reasons that Codd

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:48.448
had this notion of like,
well, look at the key,

00:09:48.448 --> 00:09:50.794
nothing but the key, and
facts related to that key.

00:09:50.794 --> 00:09:52.460
Because when you go
and fetch that data,

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:54.830
you're fetching the whole row.

00:09:54.830 --> 00:09:57.170
Columnar storage, such
as what BigQuery uses,

00:09:57.170 --> 00:10:00.350
allows us to say, I'm
interested in column one,

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:02.060
just go get column one.

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:04.667
And we don't pay a cost
for accessing null values,

00:10:04.667 --> 00:10:06.500
we don't pay a cost for
storing null values,

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:09.770
and we don't pay a cost for
accessing the other columns,

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:11.570
because we don't have to.

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:13.880
We only access
the column itself.

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:17.090
And this kind of raises the
question in my mind of, well,

00:10:17.090 --> 00:10:19.460
we can relax Codd's rules
around keys a little bit,

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:22.460
because in a columnar system,
many fields can be key-like.

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:30.750
So I think this, for me, sets up
some priorities for data models

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:32.010
and modern data warehouses.

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:34.800
When I think about systems like
BigQuery and other cloud data

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:37.490
warehouses, I think
we need to re-evaluate

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:38.490
what our priorities are.

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:39.540
And I think one of
the biggest ones

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:40.980
is make your queries
easy to write.

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:43.230
Because you want more and
more people to analyze data,

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:44.970
you want to build
more systems, so make

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:46.210
your queries easy to write.

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:46.710
All right?

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:49.200
If you can denormalize in
a way that helps you get

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:51.630
to an easy query, go for it.

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:53.400
Make that data easy to join.

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:56.250
So don't have things that
require you joining data

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:58.470
six ways, maybe just the
most important joins,

00:10:58.470 --> 00:10:59.910
the most obvious
joins, or things

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:02.740
that users can get a
lot of benefit out of.

00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:06.250
Now, if you can aid
performance by utilizing things

00:11:06.250 --> 00:11:08.180
like complex types, using
things like nesting,

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:10.330
which I think Daniel is
going to talk about a bit,

00:11:10.330 --> 00:11:11.530
this is great.

00:11:11.530 --> 00:11:14.360
We want to get performance
out of our queries,

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:16.480
but the first order
of business is

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:19.064
to make sure people
can use the data.

00:11:19.064 --> 00:11:20.480
All right, and
then make data easy

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:22.220
to update if it's
required, right?

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:24.332
So don't go overboard
on the performance

00:11:24.332 --> 00:11:25.790
or on the normalization
if it means

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:27.206
that your updates
process is going

00:11:27.206 --> 00:11:30.530
to be tremendously complicated.

00:11:30.530 --> 00:11:32.824
And finally I think parsimony
is a huge piece of this.

00:11:32.824 --> 00:11:35.240
And when I think about the
spirit of what Codd was writing

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:37.760
about in the '70s, I
think that's actually

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:38.780
a huge piece of this.

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:41.620
The relational model, in some
sense, is all about parsimony.

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:44.036
It's about making the data
model something you can look at

00:11:44.036 --> 00:11:46.489
and say like, yes, that's
like the real world.

00:11:46.489 --> 00:11:48.530
And we have the opportunity,
with modern systems,

00:11:48.530 --> 00:11:52.244
to reflect that even
more closely I think.

00:11:52.244 --> 00:11:54.660
So, Daniel, do you want to
tell us a little bit about what

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:56.826
was going on in business
intelligence as it evolved,

00:11:56.826 --> 00:11:58.704
and what's different now?

00:11:58.704 --> 00:12:00.370
DANIEL MINTZ: Yeah,
so I'm going to come

00:12:00.370 --> 00:12:02.860
at this from the
entirely different angle.

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:06.280
So Dan started at the bottom
of the stack with Codd,

00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:10.270
and the databases, the IBM
System/360, and the tapes

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:11.500
that drove it.

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:13.510
And I'm going to come
from the top of the stack,

00:12:13.510 --> 00:12:15.220
from people who are
working in business

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:17.350
and trying to understand
what the data means.

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:18.870
What it means to
their business, how

00:12:18.870 --> 00:12:22.360
do they make better
decisions with that data.

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:24.400
So, when we talk about
business intelligence,

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:26.740
this is a thing that's
been around at least

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:28.750
since the early '80s.

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:31.690
And the systems that people
used for business intelligence

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:34.997
back in the early '80s,
were monolithic systems.

00:12:34.997 --> 00:12:36.580
And they were
monolithic systems where

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:40.090
you bought the BI System that
sat on top of the database,

00:12:40.090 --> 00:12:42.340
or the data warehouse, and
you bought all those things

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:42.850
together.

00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:46.960
You had systems like Cognos,
and Business Objects,

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:48.610
MicroStrategy, Informatica.

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:50.620
Lots of those are actually
still around today.

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:53.950
Lots of Fortune 500 companies,
if you go into them,

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:56.080
you're going to see these
systems still in place.

00:12:56.080 --> 00:13:00.250
And they sat on top of the
very first columnar stores

00:13:00.250 --> 00:13:02.542
like Teradata and Oracle.

00:13:02.542 --> 00:13:04.750
And the reason that you had
to buy a monolithic stack

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:06.140
was pretty simple.

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:10.390
It was because that was
the only thing there was.

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:13.450
Systems, because they
were slow and because they

00:13:13.450 --> 00:13:17.110
were expensive, the only way
that you could query data

00:13:17.110 --> 00:13:19.360
to get information
about your business,

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:21.070
was with these
monolithic stacks.

00:13:21.070 --> 00:13:22.120
You bought all in one.

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:25.540
Why?

00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:27.123
Well, you had slow
expensive hardware.

00:13:27.123 --> 00:13:30.700
That was the defining
constraint that

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:33.760
decided what you were going
to buy and how it was built.

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:36.790
IT, which is a word
that has come to mean I

00:13:36.790 --> 00:13:39.670
think a different thing among
the newer breed of companies.

00:13:39.670 --> 00:13:42.880
It's like IT is the people
you go to fix your laptop now,

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:45.970
but IT used to be the
department that owned

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:47.847
all technology in the company.

00:13:47.847 --> 00:13:50.180
And when you were talking
about these monolithic stacks,

00:13:50.180 --> 00:13:53.800
IT was a department
that owned that stack.

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:57.790
And they owned that stack, they
probably owned the server farm

00:13:57.790 --> 00:14:03.760
where the semi backed up
with your new Hyperion data

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:08.950
warehouse, and trucked it into
the data warehouse that was

00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:13.240
actually a physical warehouse.

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:15.190
So that was another
big constraint

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:17.230
was that IT had to own
it, because they were

00:14:17.230 --> 00:14:19.900
the only people with the
technological know-how

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:21.490
to make it work.

00:14:21.490 --> 00:14:24.370
And the other
constraint was we didn't

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:27.670
have data flowing in from
every device in our pocket,

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:32.770
and every server in the
cloud, and just there's

00:14:32.770 --> 00:14:34.030
so much data now.

00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:36.490
But that was not true back
then, because data entry

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:38.180
was actual data entry.

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:41.710
There were data entry clerks
who sat and enter data.

00:14:41.710 --> 00:14:45.130
If you were managing hundreds
or thousands of stores,

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:47.710
there wasn't a
point of sale system

00:14:47.710 --> 00:14:50.950
that automatically sent data
about what had been bought back

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:51.910
to the mothership.

00:14:51.910 --> 00:14:56.050
Someone sat down every day,
or every week, or every month,

00:14:56.050 --> 00:14:57.437
and typed that in.

00:14:57.437 --> 00:14:59.020
So you just didn't
have that much data

00:14:59.020 --> 00:15:00.620
that you were dealing with.

00:15:00.620 --> 00:15:04.790
And so these systems grew
up, these first wave systems

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:06.950
grew up, and they did
have some advantages.

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:09.050
First of all, because
IT owned them,

00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:11.540
the answers that you got
out of them were reliable.

00:15:11.540 --> 00:15:14.960
There was a single
department, a single person,

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:17.060
in a lot of cases,
who owned the system,

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:20.730
and they said, yes, I stamp
this with my stamp of approval

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:22.310
and I said this data is right.

00:15:22.310 --> 00:15:25.886
And so if you were trying
to produce an SEC report,

00:15:25.886 --> 00:15:27.260
you knew that the
data and it was

00:15:27.260 --> 00:15:28.910
right because
somebody who really

00:15:28.910 --> 00:15:30.800
knew the data had blessed it.

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:33.470
They also made it really easy
to make pixel perfect stuff

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:36.310
because you were still
printing these reports out

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:37.190
in a lot of cases.

00:15:37.190 --> 00:15:40.130
You didn't have touch screens
that you could interact

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:42.139
with them on, they had
to be pixel perfect

00:15:42.139 --> 00:15:43.430
because you were printing them.

00:15:43.430 --> 00:15:47.570
And for 1980s, 1990s,
they were pretty fast.

00:15:47.570 --> 00:15:50.750
You could actually get access to
the data in a reasonable amount

00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:53.655
of time, you know, days,
not weeks or months,

00:15:53.655 --> 00:15:55.280
and you could get
your reports that you

00:15:55.280 --> 00:15:57.344
needed to run your business.

00:15:57.344 --> 00:15:58.760
But there were
some disadvantages,

00:15:58.760 --> 00:15:59.540
not surprisingly.

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:02.240
First of all, they were
terribly inflexible.

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:05.090
You'd put in a request for a
new report or a new dashboard,

00:16:05.090 --> 00:16:07.040
and first of all, you
only had the authority

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:10.190
to put on that request if you
were probably a C-level exec,

00:16:10.190 --> 00:16:12.389
and you only could
do that if you

00:16:12.389 --> 00:16:14.180
were in a giant company
that could actually

00:16:14.180 --> 00:16:16.100
afford one of these systems.

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:17.720
And so you'd put
in that request,

00:16:17.720 --> 00:16:19.970
and then you'd wait
two or three months

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:22.340
to get your new dashboard.

00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:23.750
They were locked down.

00:16:23.750 --> 00:16:26.870
People couldn't access the data,
because, my god, if you access

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:29.392
the data, it would overload
the data warehouse,

00:16:29.392 --> 00:16:31.100
and that would be
crazy, because we don't

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:32.975
want to have to spend
another million dollars

00:16:32.975 --> 00:16:36.500
to buy another appliance
to run more queries.

00:16:36.500 --> 00:16:38.090
And they were pretty
low resolution.

00:16:38.090 --> 00:16:39.680
Luckily, there wasn't
that much data,

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:42.950
so low data resolution wasn't
that big a deal, but they were.

00:16:42.950 --> 00:16:46.790
And so as the scales
of data grew over time,

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:48.790
people were not
seeing the raw data,

00:16:48.790 --> 00:16:51.230
but they were seeing
roll ups and summaries.

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:53.690
Because the only way
to make these systems

00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:55.820
continue to work on
larger sets of data,

00:16:55.820 --> 00:16:59.180
was to reduce the size of
that data by summarizing it,

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:01.610
and accessing those
in the dashboards.

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:04.339
So not surprisingly,
people started

00:17:04.339 --> 00:17:06.710
to get fed up with
these first wave tools

00:17:06.710 --> 00:17:11.119
because they weren't living
up to their evolving needs.

00:17:11.119 --> 00:17:14.599
And so in the late
'90s, early 2000s,

00:17:14.599 --> 00:17:16.730
you start to see the
second wave of Vi tools,

00:17:16.730 --> 00:17:18.710
and these are all
about self-service.

00:17:18.710 --> 00:17:20.869
You don't have to
wait for IT anymore,

00:17:20.869 --> 00:17:22.940
you can self-serve to the data.

00:17:22.940 --> 00:17:25.190
And you get this
proliferation, this explosion

00:17:25.190 --> 00:17:26.089
of different tools.

00:17:26.089 --> 00:17:28.160
Visualization tools, and
you have a governance

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:30.200
tool, over here, and
your ETL tools over here,

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:32.150
and you still get your
data engine up here,

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:34.280
and you still have your
Legacy system, which still

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:37.050
runs your SEC reports because
those have to be perfect,

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:39.830
and when we close quarter,
we use that, right?

00:17:39.830 --> 00:17:42.230
And so you just have all
of these different tools.

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:44.840
And, you know, again, why?

00:17:44.840 --> 00:17:47.770
Well first of all computers
have gotten a lot faster.

00:17:47.770 --> 00:17:51.110
You now have PCs
and even laptops

00:17:51.110 --> 00:17:54.650
that can hold a
reasonable amount of data.

00:17:54.650 --> 00:17:57.140
Not nearly as much data as
what lives in your warehouse,

00:17:57.140 --> 00:17:59.337
but you could maybe
carve off a chunk of data

00:17:59.337 --> 00:18:01.670
that you really cared about
because you were in finance,

00:18:01.670 --> 00:18:03.753
and you could carve off
some of that finance data,

00:18:03.753 --> 00:18:06.110
you'd get a hold of it,
you wouldn't give it back,

00:18:06.110 --> 00:18:08.720
and you'd load it into
a server under your desk

00:18:08.720 --> 00:18:12.170
or onto your laptop so you
could do some analysis.

00:18:12.170 --> 00:18:13.610
And your computer
was fast enough

00:18:13.610 --> 00:18:16.320
to actually slice and dice that
data in some reasonable ways

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:18.830
so you could get some
meaning out of it.

00:18:18.830 --> 00:18:21.710
People went to the second
wave because they were fed up

00:18:21.710 --> 00:18:23.180
with the IT bottleneck.

00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:25.490
They were sick of waiting
three months for a change

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:26.300
to their dashboard.

00:18:26.300 --> 00:18:28.880
I want to change the title on
dashboard, great, get in line,

00:18:28.880 --> 00:18:30.590
we'll be with you
in three months.

00:18:30.590 --> 00:18:32.090
That didn't fly anymore.

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:34.520
And, because the amount of
data that people were trying

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:36.260
to work with was
growing, and they

00:18:36.260 --> 00:18:40.430
were sick of having to do
roll ups, they said well,

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:42.770
but I only care about
this little slice of data,

00:18:42.770 --> 00:18:44.940
and I can fit that
on my computer,

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:48.720
so I want high resolution
access to just that data.

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:51.710
And so they could do that
with these new tools.

00:18:51.710 --> 00:18:54.500
So in terms of advantages, this
gave people a lot more agility

00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:57.020
than they'd ever had before,
and that was a big win.

00:18:57.020 --> 00:18:58.980
They could actually
self-serve to the data,

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:00.938
and that gave them much
faster time to insight.

00:19:00.938 --> 00:19:03.170
It meant that they weren't
waiting months and months

00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:06.890
to get any explanation,
any response.

00:19:06.890 --> 00:19:09.710
And it also gave them that
higher data resolution.

00:19:09.710 --> 00:19:12.590
But it had some disadvantages.

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:15.500
You know, I think the
tools of this era,

00:19:15.500 --> 00:19:17.690
partially by
necessity, necessitated

00:19:17.690 --> 00:19:20.270
by the technology
of the day, they

00:19:20.270 --> 00:19:22.700
made some choices
about things that they

00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:24.756
would hold onto and
keep, and things

00:19:24.756 --> 00:19:25.880
that they would throw away.

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:27.629
And one of the things
that they threw away

00:19:27.629 --> 00:19:29.960
was a shared model, a
shared understanding

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:34.100
at the business level, about
what the data actually meant.

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:37.070
That thing used to live,
in the first wave tools,

00:19:37.070 --> 00:19:39.860
in the monolithic stack and
in the way that the data was

00:19:39.860 --> 00:19:41.090
rolled up.

00:19:41.090 --> 00:19:43.190
You get these second
wave tools, people

00:19:43.190 --> 00:19:45.670
are slicing off a
little bit of the data,

00:19:45.670 --> 00:19:48.380
analyzing it in a workbook,
now all of a sudden

00:19:48.380 --> 00:19:49.940
the meaning of the
data, that shared

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:54.024
understanding of what the data
means, that gets left behind.

00:19:54.024 --> 00:19:55.940
You're also really
dependent on data extracts,

00:19:55.940 --> 00:19:58.130
and data extracts
are really troubling.

00:19:58.130 --> 00:20:00.560
Because I extracted
my data last month,

00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:02.405
and you extracted
your data last week,

00:20:02.405 --> 00:20:04.530
and now they're not in
sync, and we don't know why.

00:20:04.530 --> 00:20:06.154
And so rather than
spending the meeting

00:20:06.154 --> 00:20:08.785
discussing what our strategy
going forward should be,

00:20:08.785 --> 00:20:10.160
we spend the whole
meaning trying

00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:12.260
to debug why we're getting
different results out

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:14.210
of our data.

00:20:14.210 --> 00:20:16.790
And then the third
disadvantage was just that you

00:20:16.790 --> 00:20:18.470
had this tool explosion.

00:20:18.470 --> 00:20:21.020
You had so many tools
that people just

00:20:21.020 --> 00:20:23.420
had trouble keeping
track of, how

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:26.570
am I supposed to get this
data from here to there?

00:20:26.570 --> 00:20:29.360
And so that that created
a lot of problems.

00:20:29.360 --> 00:20:32.960
So that's from the BI
perspective what was happening.

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:38.500
At the same time, that Codd
and the data warehouses--

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:41.180
or the databases-- that turned
into columnar data warehouses

00:20:41.180 --> 00:20:43.940
were growing underneath.

00:20:43.940 --> 00:20:45.745
So let's jump forward to today.

00:20:45.745 --> 00:20:47.870
DAN MCCLARY: So we can
actually talk about the fact

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:49.370
that maybe things
are different now.

00:20:49.370 --> 00:20:50.870
Maybe we can do some new things.

00:20:50.870 --> 00:20:52.490
I think it's interesting
to point out,

00:20:52.490 --> 00:20:55.780
and worthwhile, that the
consumerization of data,

00:20:55.780 --> 00:20:59.030
that business intelligence
and reporting enabled,

00:20:59.030 --> 00:21:02.960
actually really challenges the
fact that we need good models.

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:06.332
We need a good data
model, largely relational.

00:21:06.332 --> 00:21:07.790
But we may have to
think about ways

00:21:07.790 --> 00:21:09.740
to make it easier
for consumers to get

00:21:09.740 --> 00:21:14.330
at it in ways that allow them
to have a shared understanding.

00:21:14.330 --> 00:21:16.910
Real shared understanding
both at the warehousing level,

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:18.500
and at the tool level.

00:21:18.500 --> 00:21:21.380
So it's a talk about
BigQuery, and other things,

00:21:21.380 --> 00:21:22.330
and so would be--

00:21:22.330 --> 00:21:24.830
I would be remiss if I
didn't talk about BigQuery.

00:21:24.830 --> 00:21:27.920
In part, because we think of
it as a really good example

00:21:27.920 --> 00:21:31.010
for a modern data warehouse
that's cloud native,

00:21:31.010 --> 00:21:34.220
that requires us to challenge
our modeling assumptions.

00:21:34.220 --> 00:21:36.920
Now for those of you who
don't know about BigQuery,

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:40.315
you don't know what it is,
BigQuery is Google's enterprise

00:21:40.315 --> 00:21:40.940
data warehouse.

00:21:40.940 --> 00:21:42.830
It's the warehouse
we built starting

00:21:42.830 --> 00:21:44.540
with the Dremel
project which we wrote

00:21:44.540 --> 00:21:47.010
a paper about 10 years ago.

00:21:47.010 --> 00:21:48.950
It's central to the way
Google analyzes data.

00:21:48.950 --> 00:21:51.864
And in BigQuery, we make it
available to GCP customers.

00:21:51.864 --> 00:21:53.780
And the thing about it
that's really different

00:21:53.780 --> 00:21:55.580
is, unlike systems
in which maybe I

00:21:55.580 --> 00:22:01.160
was designing for a fashionable
1970s INS system, or even

00:22:01.160 --> 00:22:04.940
the warehousing appliances
of the '80s and '90s,

00:22:04.940 --> 00:22:07.430
Dremel and BigQuery
were really designed

00:22:07.430 --> 00:22:09.892
to be query engines that
operate at a data center scale.

00:22:09.892 --> 00:22:11.600
And this means that
we can take advantage

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:13.790
of things that are
somewhat unique to GCP

00:22:13.790 --> 00:22:15.230
and unique to Google.

00:22:15.230 --> 00:22:18.260
We separate, specifically,
storage and computing.

00:22:18.260 --> 00:22:20.865
Now in many big data systems,
this would seem like anathema.

00:22:20.865 --> 00:22:22.490
This would be a real
problem, because I

00:22:22.490 --> 00:22:24.550
want to keep close to the data.

00:22:24.550 --> 00:22:27.467
However, because Google's
networks are so fast--

00:22:27.467 --> 00:22:29.300
and I encourage everybody
to take a look out

00:22:29.300 --> 00:22:32.060
on the show floor at the Jupiter
switch that we've brought,

00:22:32.060 --> 00:22:33.934
because this is really
some of the stuff that

00:22:33.934 --> 00:22:35.580
makes GCP super powerful.

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:37.650
We have a petabit
bisection in our network.

00:22:37.650 --> 00:22:39.710
Which means all
of a sudden, I can

00:22:39.710 --> 00:22:43.340
have vast amounts of compute,
that are separate from storage,

00:22:43.340 --> 00:22:47.940
but access that data as if
it were effectively local.

00:22:47.940 --> 00:22:51.090
Now this means that our query
engine can do a lot of stuff.

00:22:51.090 --> 00:22:53.205
At scale, that's really,
really different.

00:22:53.205 --> 00:22:54.580
It also means I
can start to play

00:22:54.580 --> 00:22:58.200
some really interesting games
with the way I model my data.

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:02.040
Now, the Compute Engine
itself is Dremel.

00:23:02.040 --> 00:23:03.630
It is our Dremel architecture.

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:05.340
And this is
effectively a dynamic

00:23:05.340 --> 00:23:08.400
serving tree of many, many
shards of compute workers

00:23:08.400 --> 00:23:11.130
that can apply thousands
of cores to problems.

00:23:11.130 --> 00:23:13.470
This is a dynamically
organized tree,

00:23:13.470 --> 00:23:16.057
and we are able to use the
resources per the query.

00:23:16.057 --> 00:23:18.390
Now the nice thing is that
there's an economic advantage

00:23:18.390 --> 00:23:21.054
and, so much as you only pay
for the CPU cycles you're using,

00:23:21.054 --> 00:23:22.470
but at the same
time it also means

00:23:22.470 --> 00:23:24.540
we can apply a tremendous
amount of compute

00:23:24.540 --> 00:23:27.010
to to some of these problems.

00:23:27.010 --> 00:23:29.610
It allows us to have multistage
queries that go really fast.

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:33.117
Joining's OK, joining's
safe, but at the same time

00:23:33.117 --> 00:23:34.200
it's a distributed system.

00:23:34.200 --> 00:23:36.404
And so we need to
make sure that joins

00:23:36.404 --> 00:23:38.820
are things that we want to do,
that they really make sense

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:40.719
in the context of the problem.

00:23:40.719 --> 00:23:42.510
And the other thing,
I talked about shuffle

00:23:42.510 --> 00:23:43.980
and the cost of shuffle.

00:23:43.980 --> 00:23:46.950
Well, shuffle and any
distribution system costs.

00:23:46.950 --> 00:23:50.550
However, BigQuery does a
unique thing when it shuffles.

00:23:50.550 --> 00:23:52.140
It shuffles to remote memory.

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:54.540
We wrote at a really interesting
blog about this earlier

00:23:54.540 --> 00:23:55.500
in the year.

00:23:55.500 --> 00:23:57.970
And by having a
remote shuffle layer,

00:23:57.970 --> 00:24:01.470
this allows us to handle
shuffle operations much faster,

00:24:01.470 --> 00:24:03.840
and at a higher scale
than most systems.

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:06.510
So this means that
we can join data even

00:24:06.510 --> 00:24:10.069
in situations in which the
normalization might help us.

00:24:10.069 --> 00:24:12.360
But it does mean that because
there is a shuffle stage,

00:24:12.360 --> 00:24:15.605
things like [INAUDIBLE]
can cause problems.

00:24:15.605 --> 00:24:16.980
So this is just
an example of how

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:19.530
something like a select BI
with a WHERE clause in a group,

00:24:19.530 --> 00:24:21.750
would actually move in
and out of remote shuffle.

00:24:21.750 --> 00:24:24.660
And the pipeline that you get
from a remote memory shuffle,

00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:27.900
is such that again you can
do really interesting joins.

00:24:27.900 --> 00:24:32.730
But it also means that there
is a tax when you do a join.

00:24:32.730 --> 00:24:35.960
Now, our managed storage, as I
mentioned earlier, is Columnar.

00:24:35.960 --> 00:24:39.392
And we take care of things
like durability by default.

00:24:39.392 --> 00:24:40.850
So when you put
data into BigQuery,

00:24:40.850 --> 00:24:44.960
not only is it translated
into our proprietary calendar

00:24:44.960 --> 00:24:49.010
storage format capacitor,
which is another great blog on,

00:24:49.010 --> 00:24:50.495
we store columns
in our own files,

00:24:50.495 --> 00:24:52.370
we compress these, and
encrypt them on disks.

00:24:52.370 --> 00:24:54.170
The data is always encrypted.

00:24:54.170 --> 00:24:57.350
And then we replicate
it across data centers.

00:24:57.350 --> 00:24:59.330
Your data is available
in multiple zones

00:24:59.330 --> 00:25:02.160
and resilient to failure.

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:04.362
Now this Columnar
storage allows us

00:25:04.362 --> 00:25:06.820
to do some really interesting
stuff, because not only is it

00:25:06.820 --> 00:25:10.480
Columnar, and allows us to
have many key like attributes,

00:25:10.480 --> 00:25:12.790
it also allows us to take
advantage of the fact

00:25:12.790 --> 00:25:15.040
that we can filter
on these things

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:16.870
when they're still compressed.

00:25:16.870 --> 00:25:19.510
So the notion is, I don't
have to go and pay the CPU

00:25:19.510 --> 00:25:22.210
cost to decompress
things, just to figure out

00:25:22.210 --> 00:25:24.640
how the WHERE clause applies,
I can apply it to the data

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:26.800
before I've decompressed it.

00:25:26.800 --> 00:25:28.840
Now, all of this means,
I think, a few things.

00:25:28.840 --> 00:25:31.930
Again, we can think about
having many key attributes,

00:25:31.930 --> 00:25:34.270
we can think about having
complex fields and types--

00:25:34.270 --> 00:25:38.200
because in Columnar
storage engine,

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:40.810
Columnar storage system,
that supports complex types,

00:25:40.810 --> 00:25:44.560
allows us to model data
in a different fashion.

00:25:44.560 --> 00:25:46.780
But it also allows us
to say, you know what,

00:25:46.780 --> 00:25:48.400
normalization matters to me.

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:50.170
I have a relational
model that represents

00:25:50.170 --> 00:25:53.980
my business, that represents my
world, and I need to run joins.

00:25:53.980 --> 00:25:54.810
That's OK.

00:25:54.810 --> 00:25:58.210
We can bring that, and I can
move whole hog into BigQuery,

00:25:58.210 --> 00:26:00.580
and have exactly the same
kind of querying experience

00:26:00.580 --> 00:26:02.300
I would expect.

00:26:02.300 --> 00:26:05.060
So Daniel, what was happening
and what sort of new tools

00:26:05.060 --> 00:26:09.420
have emerged in this space to
deal with the cloud experience,

00:26:09.420 --> 00:26:10.690
if you will?

00:26:10.690 --> 00:26:15.380
DANIEL MINTZ: Yeah, so we talked
about the first two waves.

00:26:15.380 --> 00:26:18.710
So let's start from the
sort of middle layer.

00:26:18.710 --> 00:26:21.320
So we've talked about the bottom
layer, the actual hardware

00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:24.020
that's driving these, the top
layer of the business side,

00:26:24.020 --> 00:26:27.830
but in the middle, you've got
this way of accessing the data

00:26:27.830 --> 00:26:29.390
in it's sort of raw form.

00:26:29.390 --> 00:26:33.080
And so, I'm sure this won't
come to news to most of you

00:26:33.080 --> 00:26:36.800
but, programming language
development looked a little bit

00:26:36.800 --> 00:26:37.750
something like this.

00:26:37.750 --> 00:26:39.530
You start with
machine code, people

00:26:39.530 --> 00:26:42.186
realized that writing ones
and zeros is kind of tedious,

00:26:42.186 --> 00:26:44.060
so they're like all
right, let's do assembly.

00:26:44.060 --> 00:26:47.060
You get Fortran,
and COBOL, basic,

00:26:47.060 --> 00:26:49.730
then C comes along
in the early '70s,

00:26:49.730 --> 00:26:51.340
this is a huge revolution.

00:26:51.340 --> 00:26:54.200
'95, it turns out, was a great
year for programming languages.

00:26:54.200 --> 00:26:58.040
PHV, JavaScript, Ruby, and
Java all appear in 1995.

00:26:58.040 --> 00:27:00.200
But you get this wide range.

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:01.970
And really, what's
happening here

00:27:01.970 --> 00:27:05.660
is that the level of abstraction
from the actual processor that

00:27:05.660 --> 00:27:08.570
is doing the work,
is increasing.

00:27:08.570 --> 00:27:14.840
Go is this amazing
language that very pithily

00:27:14.840 --> 00:27:18.290
lets you run incredibly
fast programs on tons

00:27:18.290 --> 00:27:20.390
of distributed processors.

00:27:20.390 --> 00:27:23.000
Machine code, you can't
do that because you

00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:25.600
wouldn't want to write
that in ones and zeros.

00:27:25.600 --> 00:27:29.440
On the data side, this is
kind of what it looked like.

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:31.930
So we start with data
written to files.

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:34.399
This is before you have
random access memory at all,

00:27:34.399 --> 00:27:35.440
you roll your own b-tree.

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:37.150
Codd comes along.

00:27:37.150 --> 00:27:40.030
Then in the late '70s, we
get SQL, which is really

00:27:40.030 --> 00:27:42.262
an operationalization.

00:27:42.262 --> 00:27:42.970
Wow, that's hard.

00:27:42.970 --> 00:27:44.020
Operationalization.

00:27:44.020 --> 00:27:45.570
I'm curious what the--

00:27:45.570 --> 00:27:47.020
yeah, the robot got it right.

00:27:47.020 --> 00:27:51.490
The robot that is transcribing
got it right, amazingly.

00:27:51.490 --> 00:27:53.740
So you get SQL shows up
in the late '70s, that's

00:27:53.740 --> 00:27:57.780
an operationalization of
Codd's rules, Codd's laws.

00:27:57.780 --> 00:28:00.310
And then you get Oracle
and IBM, which turn them

00:28:00.310 --> 00:28:02.070
into commercial software.

00:28:02.070 --> 00:28:05.980
In the late '80s you get the
T-SQL, which is still SQL.

00:28:05.980 --> 00:28:10.330
And the '90s, you get MySQL and
PostgreSQL, which are still SQL

00:28:10.330 --> 00:28:11.830
fundamentally.

00:28:11.830 --> 00:28:14.560
And really, there hasn't
been very much development

00:28:14.560 --> 00:28:15.315
on the data side.

00:28:15.315 --> 00:28:17.440
So at the same time that
programming languages have

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:20.620
been evolving
enormously, data language

00:28:20.620 --> 00:28:23.770
has been kind of stuck.

00:28:23.770 --> 00:28:27.940
And so in terms of
what we actually want,

00:28:27.940 --> 00:28:31.090
we wanted to define
relationships and definitions

00:28:31.090 --> 00:28:31.897
once.

00:28:31.897 --> 00:28:33.730
We don't want to have
to do that repeatedly.

00:28:33.730 --> 00:28:35.830
Computers are good at
doing tasks repeatedly,

00:28:35.830 --> 00:28:38.670
humans like to do it
once and be done with it.

00:28:38.670 --> 00:28:40.710
We want to retain
all of the agility

00:28:40.710 --> 00:28:42.375
that SQL gives us,
which is amazing.

00:28:45.380 --> 00:28:48.560
We want an easy way of
translating what we actually

00:28:48.560 --> 00:28:51.440
mean, the business question,
the question that we care about,

00:28:51.440 --> 00:28:52.820
into a data query.

00:28:52.820 --> 00:28:56.060
And sending that off to
the database or the data

00:28:56.060 --> 00:28:58.580
warehouse so we can get that
data that answers the business

00:28:58.580 --> 00:29:00.020
question back.

00:29:00.020 --> 00:29:02.420
We want to state performant,
which is becoming

00:29:02.420 --> 00:29:03.620
less and less of a problem.

00:29:03.620 --> 00:29:06.350
But still, when you're querying
petabytes of data still

00:29:06.350 --> 00:29:08.060
is an issue.

00:29:08.060 --> 00:29:11.570
And we don't really want
to worry about syntax.

00:29:11.570 --> 00:29:15.266
Because, you know, the
proliferation of SQLs,

00:29:15.266 --> 00:29:16.640
means that you
have to keep track

00:29:16.640 --> 00:29:20.240
of little bits of, oh, this
is how this one handles date

00:29:20.240 --> 00:29:22.730
reformatting, and
humans shouldn't have

00:29:22.730 --> 00:29:23.930
to worry about that, right?

00:29:23.930 --> 00:29:25.550
Computer programmers
figured that out.

00:29:25.550 --> 00:29:28.580
They said, we don't want
to worry about that stuff.

00:29:28.580 --> 00:29:31.540
I don't need to worry
about memory management

00:29:31.540 --> 00:29:35.949
except in 1% of the cases, so
I won't use C most of the time,

00:29:35.949 --> 00:29:38.240
I'll use something that takes
care of memory management

00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:40.250
and garbage collection for me.

00:29:40.250 --> 00:29:42.050
Analysts are still
stuck writing SQL where

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:43.790
they're worrying about syntax.

00:29:43.790 --> 00:29:45.890
They're doing a lot
of these things.

00:29:45.890 --> 00:29:50.450
And so, lots of analysts I
talked to say, but I love SQL.

00:29:50.450 --> 00:29:53.240
And I love SQL, too.

00:29:53.240 --> 00:29:54.380
Dan loves SQL, too.

00:29:54.380 --> 00:29:57.030
Does anybody else love SQL?

00:29:57.030 --> 00:29:58.640
Yeah, SQL's amazing.

00:29:58.640 --> 00:30:01.494
I am getting a lot of like, meh.

00:30:01.494 --> 00:30:03.660
Yeah, I have a love hate
relationship with SQL, too.

00:30:03.660 --> 00:30:05.430
That basically describes it.

00:30:05.430 --> 00:30:06.590
So SQL's amazing.

00:30:06.590 --> 00:30:09.840
It's proven it's powerful, it's
versatile, it's everywhere.

00:30:09.840 --> 00:30:13.890
SQL and C fundamentally
run the world, right?

00:30:13.890 --> 00:30:17.910
But, SQL's really
easy to screw up.

00:30:17.910 --> 00:30:20.730
In my first job, where
I was writing SQL,

00:30:20.730 --> 00:30:23.850
in the Orders table there
was the status field.

00:30:23.850 --> 00:30:25.260
And you had to say Orders.

00:30:25.260 --> 00:30:26.882
Status equals Completed.

00:30:26.882 --> 00:30:29.340
And if you didn't, you would
get all the failed credit card

00:30:29.340 --> 00:30:32.160
transactions returned
as part of your query.

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:34.717
And you go, oh my god
we had an amazing day,

00:30:34.717 --> 00:30:37.050
but you didn't, you just had
a lot of failed credit card

00:30:37.050 --> 00:30:38.880
transactions.

00:30:38.880 --> 00:30:42.210
And so keeping track of, oh, I
have to put the group by there,

00:30:42.210 --> 00:30:45.180
and I have to-- what
dialect am I in,

00:30:45.180 --> 00:30:48.750
how does it handle date
conversions, that's stuff

00:30:48.750 --> 00:30:50.380
that programmers
have gotten away from

00:30:50.380 --> 00:30:52.720
and analysts are
still stuck with.

00:30:52.720 --> 00:30:56.310
And so what Looker has done--

00:30:56.310 --> 00:30:59.310
and I should say I was a
Looker customer for far longer

00:30:59.310 --> 00:31:02.460
than I have worked for Looker.

00:31:02.460 --> 00:31:06.210
And so I've stumbled on Looker
early on in Looker's history,

00:31:06.210 --> 00:31:08.580
and they made this promise,
and I said, oh my god,

00:31:08.580 --> 00:31:10.610
I want this thing,
but it doesn't--

00:31:10.610 --> 00:31:13.794
it kind of exists, maybe it
won't really live up to it--

00:31:13.794 --> 00:31:15.210
and it totally has
lived up to it,

00:31:15.210 --> 00:31:17.168
and that's why I sort of
jumped over the fence.

00:31:17.168 --> 00:31:20.010
But Looker started with this
kernel of this idea of LookML,

00:31:20.010 --> 00:31:22.890
which is SQL, evolved.

00:31:22.890 --> 00:31:25.350
It says, let's stop worrying
about all that stuff.

00:31:25.350 --> 00:31:30.000
And so it makes this data
language reusable, which

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:31.680
is not a thing that SQL is.

00:31:31.680 --> 00:31:34.110
I know when I go look at
SQL I wrote two weeks ago,

00:31:34.110 --> 00:31:36.429
I give up immediately.

00:31:36.429 --> 00:31:38.470
I'm like, oh, I'll write
this from scratch again,

00:31:38.470 --> 00:31:41.670
because I had no idea
what I was doing.

00:31:41.670 --> 00:31:44.070
It makes data language
collaborative.

00:31:44.070 --> 00:31:45.540
Because if I try
reading something

00:31:45.540 --> 00:31:47.290
I wrote two weeks ago,
something Dan wrote

00:31:47.290 --> 00:31:49.980
is probably even worse and
harder to understand for me,

00:31:49.980 --> 00:31:52.440
because he has his own
style of writing it.

00:31:52.440 --> 00:31:55.290
No offense to the way
that Dan writes SQL.

00:31:55.290 --> 00:31:57.820
I'm sure it's great.

00:31:57.820 --> 00:32:00.490
It makes data language flexible.

00:32:00.490 --> 00:32:03.600
It makes it easy to organize
so you don't have untitled,

00:32:03.600 --> 00:32:06.810
underscore, 43, dot SQL,
untitled, underscore,

00:32:06.810 --> 00:32:08.550
44, dot SQL.

00:32:08.550 --> 00:32:10.360
These little recipes
on your desktop.

00:32:10.360 --> 00:32:12.006
I definitely have those.

00:32:12.006 --> 00:32:13.380
And it gives you
version control,

00:32:13.380 --> 00:32:15.671
which is another thing that
programmers a long time ago

00:32:15.671 --> 00:32:17.282
were like, hey,
maybe we should keep

00:32:17.282 --> 00:32:19.740
track of what we did in the
past, so if we need to go back,

00:32:19.740 --> 00:32:20.970
we can.

00:32:20.970 --> 00:32:22.152
Data people, not so much.

00:32:22.152 --> 00:32:23.610
And the way that
it does this is it

00:32:23.610 --> 00:32:28.240
says, well, really any query can
be decomposed into four things.

00:32:28.240 --> 00:32:31.600
One is a set of
relationships between tables.

00:32:31.600 --> 00:32:34.470
The second is the fields
that you actually want.

00:32:34.470 --> 00:32:37.410
The third is what filters you
want to apply to the data.

00:32:37.410 --> 00:32:39.354
And the fourth is how
you want to sort it.

00:32:39.354 --> 00:32:40.770
And if you have
those four things,

00:32:40.770 --> 00:32:43.620
you can really
compose any query.

00:32:43.620 --> 00:32:46.770
A computer could compile those
four things down into a SQL

00:32:46.770 --> 00:32:48.040
and send it off to a database.

00:32:48.040 --> 00:32:50.970
And so that's what
LookML is, and that's

00:32:50.970 --> 00:32:56.040
what allows you to build a
sort of third wave of BI.

00:32:56.040 --> 00:32:59.220
Which is a data
platform that says,

00:32:59.220 --> 00:33:01.380
you know what,
we'll have the data

00:33:01.380 --> 00:33:04.170
platform take care of
those annoying things

00:33:04.170 --> 00:33:07.080
that we've always worried about,
and allow people, whether they

00:33:07.080 --> 00:33:11.580
speak SQL or not, to
come in and write queries

00:33:11.580 --> 00:33:13.590
without looking at the
SQL, without needing

00:33:13.590 --> 00:33:15.870
to look at the SQL, write
queries against the data.

00:33:15.870 --> 00:33:21.780
And that allows you to
access the data directly.

00:33:21.780 --> 00:33:24.990
And it's only possible because
of this data infrastructure

00:33:24.990 --> 00:33:26.760
revolution that's
happened underneath.

00:33:26.760 --> 00:33:29.629
I think big data
is a lot of hype.

00:33:29.629 --> 00:33:31.920
The big data revolution has
been a lot of hype, and not

00:33:31.920 --> 00:33:33.940
that much delivery quite yet.

00:33:33.940 --> 00:33:36.300
But one thing it
absolutely did deliver on,

00:33:36.300 --> 00:33:39.420
is enormously fast databases.

00:33:39.420 --> 00:33:42.330
You go back even
five or 10 years,

00:33:42.330 --> 00:33:47.100
when Hadoop was going to be
the answer, how far we've

00:33:47.100 --> 00:33:48.750
come from then of
like, well, maybe

00:33:48.750 --> 00:33:50.730
we should shard MySQL a lot.

00:33:50.730 --> 00:33:51.930
Ooh, that's really ugly.

00:33:51.930 --> 00:33:53.040
Let's use Hadoop.

00:33:53.040 --> 00:33:55.090
Oh, boy-- you know?

00:33:55.090 --> 00:33:57.300
Hadoop was kind of going
back to the old days where

00:33:57.300 --> 00:33:59.524
you'd like type in the
program at night at 11:00 PM

00:33:59.524 --> 00:34:01.190
and then you come
back the next morning,

00:34:01.190 --> 00:34:03.540
and aw, man I had a typo.

00:34:03.540 --> 00:34:06.360
And you'd have to rerun
it the next night.

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:08.040
It was very much
going backwards.

00:34:08.040 --> 00:34:11.250
So these incredibly
fast databases,

00:34:11.250 --> 00:34:13.380
that mostly live
in the cloud, have

00:34:13.380 --> 00:34:14.790
made this revolution possible.

00:34:14.790 --> 00:34:16.248
Because all of a
sudden, you didn't

00:34:16.248 --> 00:34:18.187
have to do all the preparation.

00:34:18.187 --> 00:34:19.770
You could actually
set a data platform

00:34:19.770 --> 00:34:22.469
on top of these databases
and just access the data,

00:34:22.469 --> 00:34:25.469
leveraging all of their power.

00:34:25.469 --> 00:34:29.100
And so, in this third
wave, well, first of all.

00:34:29.100 --> 00:34:32.159
It was only possible because
the databases were that fast.

00:34:32.159 --> 00:34:33.989
Without these databases,
it doesn't work.

00:34:36.670 --> 00:34:38.670
One of the other reasons
that people wanted this

00:34:38.670 --> 00:34:41.520
was because they were just
sick of having a million tools.

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:44.909
If you could centralize
all this work in one tool,

00:34:44.909 --> 00:34:46.570
that would make
life a lot easier.

00:34:46.570 --> 00:34:48.750
And the third, as
Dan talked about, I

00:34:48.750 --> 00:34:52.679
don't know if you guys noticed,
but the x-axes of those two

00:34:52.679 --> 00:34:55.860
price drop graphs, one started
in 1980, the other only

00:34:55.860 --> 00:34:57.480
started in 2000.

00:34:57.480 --> 00:35:00.480
And that's because this
idea of shuffling data

00:35:00.480 --> 00:35:03.360
across a network, simply
didn't exist in the old days.

00:35:03.360 --> 00:35:07.800
But now that it does,
you can access the data

00:35:07.800 --> 00:35:09.630
in these incredibly
fast databases which

00:35:09.630 --> 00:35:12.900
live in the cloud, and then
bring it to your machine.

00:35:12.900 --> 00:35:15.990
And so in terms of
advantages, well

00:35:15.990 --> 00:35:18.891
the third wave means you're
getting reliable answers.

00:35:18.891 --> 00:35:20.640
And not just reliable
answers in the sense

00:35:20.640 --> 00:35:23.250
that the data is consistent, but
reliable answers in the sense

00:35:23.250 --> 00:35:26.520
that someone who knows what
the data is supposed to mean,

00:35:26.520 --> 00:35:32.430
went in, and said this is
the definition of our KPI.

00:35:32.430 --> 00:35:35.040
This is the definition of
average contract value,

00:35:35.040 --> 00:35:38.130
and it's a plus b, divided by c.

00:35:38.130 --> 00:35:39.810
And so when you want
to access that, you

00:35:39.810 --> 00:35:43.350
don't have to remember oh,
wait, was it a plus b, minus c,

00:35:43.350 --> 00:35:45.126
or over--

00:35:45.126 --> 00:35:46.000
that's in the system.

00:35:46.000 --> 00:35:48.176
The system worries
about that for you.

00:35:48.176 --> 00:35:49.800
It gives you agility
because you're not

00:35:49.800 --> 00:35:52.620
constrained by the data
that lives on your machine,

00:35:52.620 --> 00:35:54.960
you're accessing the data
right where it lives.

00:35:54.960 --> 00:35:57.510
And so you can
access all the data.

00:35:57.510 --> 00:36:00.450
It lets you pick the
tools that are right.

00:36:00.450 --> 00:36:04.900
So you choose what is the right
amazing cloud database for you?

00:36:04.900 --> 00:36:06.856
What is the right
ETL tool for you?

00:36:06.856 --> 00:36:08.730
What is the right
visualization tool for you?

00:36:08.730 --> 00:36:11.070
You're not locked into
these monolithic systems.

00:36:11.070 --> 00:36:12.599
And it gives you
full resolution.

00:36:12.599 --> 00:36:14.640
You're not rolling up the
data and summarizing it

00:36:14.640 --> 00:36:17.580
before you access
it, you can always

00:36:17.580 --> 00:36:19.440
drill down right
to the lowest row,

00:36:19.440 --> 00:36:22.350
because you send a billion
row or 100 billion row

00:36:22.350 --> 00:36:24.900
query to BigQuery
and BigQuery says,

00:36:24.900 --> 00:36:28.240
all right, I'll
be right with you.

00:36:28.240 --> 00:36:29.800
There are some disadvantages.

00:36:29.800 --> 00:36:31.900
First, is that it's a
major shift in thinking,

00:36:31.900 --> 00:36:34.000
especially for the
people who worked in IT

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:36.125
when it was called IT.

00:36:36.125 --> 00:36:37.000
It's like a big deal.

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:38.874
They have to let go.

00:36:38.874 --> 00:36:40.540
And that's hard for
a lot of businesses,

00:36:40.540 --> 00:36:42.550
because these business
processes are deeply

00:36:42.550 --> 00:36:44.260
embedded in the organizations.

00:36:44.260 --> 00:36:46.240
And so for
organizations to think

00:36:46.240 --> 00:36:48.220
of how they can use
data in these new ways

00:36:48.220 --> 00:36:50.590
is actually pretty hard.

00:36:50.590 --> 00:36:52.210
You do need a powerful
data warehouse.

00:36:52.210 --> 00:36:57.190
If you've got an IBM
System/360, the third wave of BI

00:36:57.190 --> 00:36:57.940
is not for you.

00:36:57.940 --> 00:37:00.195
Don't do that .

00:37:00.195 --> 00:37:02.320
And this is something that
we've only begun to see,

00:37:02.320 --> 00:37:06.394
but all of a sudden you have
insight coming from everywhere.

00:37:06.394 --> 00:37:07.810
Because the people
who are closest

00:37:07.810 --> 00:37:09.976
to what the data actually
means, the business people

00:37:09.976 --> 00:37:14.380
who understand what it means,
are able to explore freely,

00:37:14.380 --> 00:37:17.620
and so they're finding stuff
that they couldn't find before.

00:37:17.620 --> 00:37:21.340
And so going from information
scarcity to insight abundance,

00:37:21.340 --> 00:37:23.620
is actually hard in some cases.

00:37:23.620 --> 00:37:27.177
It's the same thing of going
from a bunch of word docs

00:37:27.177 --> 00:37:29.260
on your machine, now you're
collaborating on stuff

00:37:29.260 --> 00:37:31.480
with a bunch of people,
you need Google Drive

00:37:31.480 --> 00:37:35.140
because you need a way to
keep all that stuff organized.

00:37:35.140 --> 00:37:36.100
Otherwise you're stuck.

00:37:36.100 --> 00:37:37.806
Oh, no, you're working
on that version.

00:37:37.806 --> 00:37:39.430
I already made changes
to that version,

00:37:39.430 --> 00:37:41.380
I emailed-- did you
not see the email?

00:37:41.380 --> 00:37:44.110
You don't want to
go down that road.

00:37:44.110 --> 00:37:48.790
So that is a new way of
thinking, but this is possible.

00:37:48.790 --> 00:37:53.500
So now that we've bored you
all with the very high level

00:37:53.500 --> 00:37:56.700
abstract, you've lived through
the data model section,

00:37:56.700 --> 00:37:58.390
now we're going
to get practical.

00:37:58.390 --> 00:38:01.000
And talk about what it means to
model your data in this brave

00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:01.841
new world.

00:38:01.841 --> 00:38:04.090
DAN MCCLARY: Wait, wait, I
had one more academic point

00:38:04.090 --> 00:38:04.740
to make.

00:38:04.740 --> 00:38:06.280
DANIEL MINTZ: Sorry, I lied.

00:38:06.280 --> 00:38:08.980
DAN MCCLARY: I know, I know,
I just can't give it up.

00:38:08.980 --> 00:38:12.610
Which is though, when we think
about the spirit of Codd's

00:38:12.610 --> 00:38:16.630
original work, and the notion
that a relational algebra is

00:38:16.630 --> 00:38:21.070
a powerful tool to apply to
modeling real world situations,

00:38:21.070 --> 00:38:23.740
the business user needs it,
too, but for the business user,

00:38:23.740 --> 00:38:26.380
semantics and context
matter as well.

00:38:26.380 --> 00:38:28.780
It's not it's not
simply the set theory

00:38:28.780 --> 00:38:31.150
or the set theoretic
operations I want to perform,

00:38:31.150 --> 00:38:34.282
it's that these have
specific in-context meaning.

00:38:34.282 --> 00:38:35.740
And so I think a
lot of what we see

00:38:35.740 --> 00:38:39.220
and the interplay between
classic data modeling and data

00:38:39.220 --> 00:38:41.950
modeling as it evolves
for the BI user,

00:38:41.950 --> 00:38:47.500
is that context is brought in
line with the relational model.

00:38:47.500 --> 00:38:50.040
DANIEL MINTZ: And doing that I
think is enormously valuable,

00:38:50.040 --> 00:38:52.960
and people sometimes miss
that, because the people who

00:38:52.960 --> 00:38:57.315
actually in the store,
running the POS system,

00:38:57.315 --> 00:38:59.440
they're the ones who could
look at the data and go,

00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:01.090
something's wrong here.

00:39:01.090 --> 00:39:03.070
A data analyst
back at HQ is going

00:39:03.070 --> 00:39:06.280
to miss that, because they don't
have that tactile feel for what

00:39:06.280 --> 00:39:08.160
the data should be, what
it should look like.

00:39:08.160 --> 00:39:10.960
They're going to miss the
insights if the data has

00:39:10.960 --> 00:39:13.000
to be shipped off
to HQ, and then

00:39:13.000 --> 00:39:15.640
an analyst has to groom it
and put it in the system,

00:39:15.640 --> 00:39:18.206
if they can actually
access the data themselves,

00:39:18.206 --> 00:39:20.830
they're the ones that are going
to come up with those insights.

00:39:20.830 --> 00:39:22.660
DAN MCCLARY: Outliers look
different the further away

00:39:22.660 --> 00:39:23.162
you are.

00:39:23.162 --> 00:39:24.870
DANIEL MINTZ: That's
exactly right, yeah.

00:39:24.870 --> 00:39:26.170
DAN MCCLARY: OK,
so now we can be

00:39:26.170 --> 00:39:27.640
now we can be a little
bit more practical.

00:39:27.640 --> 00:39:30.130
And unfortunately, I think
the one thing to sort of tell

00:39:30.130 --> 00:39:34.600
everybody, is that there's no
one hard and fast rule for,

00:39:34.600 --> 00:39:36.730
thou shalt model
your data this way.

00:39:36.730 --> 00:39:38.800
All we can do is talk
through some of the things

00:39:38.800 --> 00:39:41.440
that we see, some of the
questions that Daniel I have

00:39:41.440 --> 00:39:46.360
been asked, and what we think
are reasonable rules of thumb

00:39:46.360 --> 00:39:48.479
to apply.

00:39:48.479 --> 00:39:50.020
All right, so one
of the things I get

00:39:50.020 --> 00:39:52.840
asked a lot is, I'm really
worried about denormalizing

00:39:52.840 --> 00:39:53.410
my data.

00:39:53.410 --> 00:39:57.700
I've heard systems like BigQuery
really like denormalization.

00:39:57.700 --> 00:40:00.130
I don't know, I
have this dimension,

00:40:00.130 --> 00:40:04.010
should I put it in the
multiple fact tables?

00:40:04.010 --> 00:40:05.570
The answer is, of course, maybe.

00:40:05.570 --> 00:40:08.500
Everybody's least
favorite answer, maybe.

00:40:08.500 --> 00:40:10.480
Now the questions you
have to ask yourself

00:40:10.480 --> 00:40:12.250
are actually about
the dimension.

00:40:12.250 --> 00:40:15.760
Is this a static dimension, is
it way smaller than the fact?

00:40:15.760 --> 00:40:19.180
Is the dimension just a
list of the 50 US states?

00:40:19.180 --> 00:40:22.360
You could probably denormalize
that pretty safely.

00:40:22.360 --> 00:40:24.460
Now, if the dimension
is something

00:40:24.460 --> 00:40:28.720
that changes, or is used
independent of the facts,

00:40:28.720 --> 00:40:31.510
if I have a dimension table
it's quite large and used

00:40:31.510 --> 00:40:34.750
by many, many different
fact tables, the challenge

00:40:34.750 --> 00:40:38.020
of denormalizing it, whether
as a nested structure,

00:40:38.020 --> 00:40:41.320
or into every table is
a flat sort of columns,

00:40:41.320 --> 00:40:42.430
is quite large.

00:40:42.430 --> 00:40:45.850
So it might be better
left normalized,

00:40:45.850 --> 00:40:48.520
because that actually
models the world better.

00:40:48.520 --> 00:40:49.750
It models the world.

00:40:49.750 --> 00:40:52.810
And large scalable joins are
entirely possible with systems

00:40:52.810 --> 00:40:53.830
like BigQuery.

00:40:53.830 --> 00:40:56.420
So again, I think this notion
that I have a dimension table

00:40:56.420 --> 00:40:58.780
and I really, really want
to know whether or not

00:40:58.780 --> 00:41:00.280
I should put up
with the fact table,

00:41:00.280 --> 00:41:02.620
should I denormalize
it, well how is it used?

00:41:02.620 --> 00:41:03.850
How is it updated?

00:41:03.850 --> 00:41:05.010
How big is it?

00:41:05.010 --> 00:41:07.210
These are the sort of
fundamental questions

00:41:07.210 --> 00:41:10.340
we have to ask.

00:41:10.340 --> 00:41:12.390
So another one I
get asked a lot,

00:41:12.390 --> 00:41:14.870
and I think comes up a lot
when people come to BigQuery,

00:41:14.870 --> 00:41:18.860
they look at our type system
and they say, OK, wait a second,

00:41:18.860 --> 00:41:21.770
you've got a arrays
and structures,

00:41:21.770 --> 00:41:25.040
you have these nested
and repeated fields.

00:41:25.040 --> 00:41:27.300
What do I do with those again?

00:41:27.300 --> 00:41:29.510
Is everything now
an array of struts?

00:41:29.510 --> 00:41:32.054
What am I supposed to do there?

00:41:32.054 --> 00:41:33.470
Now, the reality
of it is, I think

00:41:33.470 --> 00:41:35.136
Daniel, you've got
some thoughts on this

00:41:35.136 --> 00:41:36.770
that you want to
share in a bit, is

00:41:36.770 --> 00:41:39.410
that nested and
repeated fields are

00:41:39.410 --> 00:41:43.040
hugely powerful
ways of preserving

00:41:43.040 --> 00:41:47.240
the logic, or the
logical relational view,

00:41:47.240 --> 00:41:50.450
while getting the physical
benefits of dennormalization.

00:41:50.450 --> 00:41:54.920
So if I have a
dimension table that

00:41:54.920 --> 00:41:59.000
represents a one to
many relationship,

00:41:59.000 --> 00:42:03.180
I can take that dimension
table that is the many,

00:42:03.180 --> 00:42:06.650
and embed it, as nested
and repeated fields,

00:42:06.650 --> 00:42:10.850
into that table and maintain
the logical view of, oh, yes,

00:42:10.850 --> 00:42:13.600
this order has a
list of items in it.

00:42:13.600 --> 00:42:16.391
OK, that's reasonable.

00:42:16.391 --> 00:42:17.390
But there is a question.

00:42:17.390 --> 00:42:19.962
So that repeated data needs
to be updated frequently,

00:42:19.962 --> 00:42:21.170
that could be a real problem.

00:42:21.170 --> 00:42:24.630
So to say the shopping
basket had these items in it,

00:42:24.630 --> 00:42:25.130
that's fine.

00:42:25.130 --> 00:42:26.630
Because that happened once.

00:42:26.630 --> 00:42:29.900
To say that I'm going
to have a nesting of all

00:42:29.900 --> 00:42:31.910
of the transactions
going on forever,

00:42:31.910 --> 00:42:32.960
becomes a real challenge.

00:42:32.960 --> 00:42:35.060
Because now I'm saying,
oh, I have an array

00:42:35.060 --> 00:42:37.970
that I'm going to modify
on every row, every day,

00:42:37.970 --> 00:42:39.260
with all of the new data.

00:42:39.260 --> 00:42:41.720
That's probably not the
right thing to go and nest.

00:42:41.720 --> 00:42:44.540
DANIEL MINTZ: I mean or
even to go even simpler than

00:42:44.540 --> 00:42:49.934
that, items are something
that belong to the order,

00:42:49.934 --> 00:42:51.350
but orders aren't
really something

00:42:51.350 --> 00:42:52.433
that belonged to the user.

00:42:52.433 --> 00:42:54.390
The user can exist
separate from that.

00:42:54.390 --> 00:42:57.260
And so the idea that, oh,
I need to go into the users

00:42:57.260 --> 00:42:59.480
table, which then
contains the orders, which

00:42:59.480 --> 00:43:01.700
contains the items, every
time that this user makes

00:43:01.700 --> 00:43:03.470
a new order--

00:43:03.470 --> 00:43:04.009
yeah.

00:43:04.009 --> 00:43:06.300
DAN MCCLARY: Well it creates
it creates a real problem.

00:43:06.300 --> 00:43:08.390
Then also in terms of
trying to reflect the world.

00:43:08.390 --> 00:43:10.764
If we want the people who were
writing reports and people

00:43:10.764 --> 00:43:14.120
were analyzing data to be
able to write queries or build

00:43:14.120 --> 00:43:16.910
reports that model
the world, we have

00:43:16.910 --> 00:43:19.520
to make sure that we're not
sort of overly denormalizing

00:43:19.520 --> 00:43:21.260
for the sake of
performance, and as such,

00:43:21.260 --> 00:43:22.770
breaking our view of reality.

00:43:22.770 --> 00:43:23.720
DANIEL MINTZ: Yeah.

00:43:23.720 --> 00:43:25.160
DAN MCCLARY: And
also if you have

00:43:25.160 --> 00:43:27.500
to, as an analyst write SQL,
you would like it to not

00:43:27.500 --> 00:43:28.910
be terribly complicated.

00:43:28.910 --> 00:43:31.490
Why do we have to do an unnest
on the orders in the user

00:43:31.490 --> 00:43:32.940
to get to the thing?

00:43:32.940 --> 00:43:34.130
That's not what we want.

00:43:34.130 --> 00:43:36.009
So when do we think
about nesting?

00:43:36.009 --> 00:43:38.300
Daniel, you've got more
thoughts you're going to share,

00:43:38.300 --> 00:43:40.365
as I said, but
again, sometimes it

00:43:40.365 --> 00:43:41.990
makes sense if we're
trying to preserve

00:43:41.990 --> 00:43:43.670
the logical relationship,
because it's

00:43:43.670 --> 00:43:45.080
important relationship.

00:43:45.080 --> 00:43:46.790
But we shouldn't just
do it because we're

00:43:46.790 --> 00:43:49.462
blindly seeking performance or
because I read the BigQuery doc

00:43:49.462 --> 00:43:51.170
and it's like, oh,
well they have Nested,

00:43:51.170 --> 00:43:54.170
I better figure out
how to use Nested.

00:43:54.170 --> 00:43:56.206
So one of the
things I see a lot,

00:43:56.206 --> 00:43:58.580
particularly with enterprises
who are coming to big worry

00:43:58.580 --> 00:44:01.760
from older systems,
is that you may

00:44:01.760 --> 00:44:03.500
have a query that
has many, many stages

00:44:03.500 --> 00:44:06.620
or you're used to running
it on your teradata machine,

00:44:06.620 --> 00:44:10.439
and it runs for two days,
and then it shows up.

00:44:10.439 --> 00:44:12.230
I feel like this is
one of those situations

00:44:12.230 --> 00:44:14.230
in which you really do
have to kind of challenge

00:44:14.230 --> 00:44:17.265
your assumption, but not
necessarily your assumptions

00:44:17.265 --> 00:44:18.140
about the data model.

00:44:18.140 --> 00:44:19.520
The model may be fine.

00:44:19.520 --> 00:44:22.196
There may be tweaks
you can make,

00:44:22.196 --> 00:44:24.320
but you have to think of
the workflow a little bit.

00:44:24.320 --> 00:44:26.840
So sometimes when we run
these queries where we say,

00:44:26.840 --> 00:44:29.240
I'm going to submit it,
I'll come back on Wednesday

00:44:29.240 --> 00:44:30.860
and we'll see what happened.

00:44:30.860 --> 00:44:32.443
Some of that's because
you're actually

00:44:32.443 --> 00:44:34.550
operating in a resource
constrained environment.

00:44:34.550 --> 00:44:36.530
You may have gotten
used to it, but it is

00:44:36.530 --> 00:44:37.460
kind of resource constrained.

00:44:37.460 --> 00:44:39.293
I'm only getting a
little bit of batch work,

00:44:39.293 --> 00:44:42.050
and I can submit it
in these time periods.

00:44:42.050 --> 00:44:45.020
And I think the challenge that

00:44:45.020 --> 00:44:48.047
I ask people to push on
that workload is, well,

00:44:48.047 --> 00:44:49.130
what if you broke it down.

00:44:49.130 --> 00:44:51.710
Are there materializations
that can preserve work?

00:44:51.710 --> 00:44:54.950
Remember, at the top of the
talk, we talked about the fact

00:44:54.950 --> 00:44:57.020
that the cost of
storage has plummeted,

00:44:57.020 --> 00:44:59.390
such that duplicate data
doesn't really cost that much,

00:44:59.390 --> 00:45:02.020
particularly if it doesn't
live for very long.

00:45:02.020 --> 00:45:04.430
So if you intermediate
materializations,

00:45:04.430 --> 00:45:06.500
you can not only sort
of preserve your work,

00:45:06.500 --> 00:45:10.010
and maybe speed up the way
you compute things over time,

00:45:10.010 --> 00:45:12.350
but you could also
think, hey, maybe there's

00:45:12.350 --> 00:45:15.770
a broad intermediate form
of either denormalized one,

00:45:15.770 --> 00:45:17.900
or a set of normalized
relationships,

00:45:17.900 --> 00:45:21.194
that are actually really useful
for a broad set of people.

00:45:21.194 --> 00:45:22.610
If I have a number
of analysts who

00:45:22.610 --> 00:45:24.860
are looking at the
same kind of things,

00:45:24.860 --> 00:45:26.870
maybe I can break my
work down into sort

00:45:26.870 --> 00:45:29.990
of a large materialization
that many analysts can then

00:45:29.990 --> 00:45:34.220
go to their leading
edge computations on.

00:45:34.220 --> 00:45:35.761
And so, one of the
things I also see

00:45:35.761 --> 00:45:37.260
is this notion of
like, oh, well I'm

00:45:37.260 --> 00:45:39.540
coming from the big data space.

00:45:39.540 --> 00:45:44.700
I suffered through MapReduce,
we got to Hive, well it's OK.

00:45:44.700 --> 00:45:46.489
It's OK.

00:45:46.489 --> 00:45:48.780
But one of the things that
really worked for me in Hive

00:45:48.780 --> 00:45:50.400
was how we partitioned our data.

00:45:50.400 --> 00:45:54.360
We had multiple levels of
list partitioning, it was OK.

00:45:54.360 --> 00:45:57.310
We were able to go
a little bit faster.

00:45:57.310 --> 00:45:59.787
But one of the things that I
think is worth pointing out

00:45:59.787 --> 00:46:01.620
is, not only does
BigQuery offer the ability

00:46:01.620 --> 00:46:04.290
to date partition tables,
which is pretty common in data

00:46:04.290 --> 00:46:07.380
warehouses, but we have
this sort of table sharding

00:46:07.380 --> 00:46:10.530
which allows you to
effectively have prefixes that

00:46:10.530 --> 00:46:13.110
are common amongst
tables of common schema,

00:46:13.110 --> 00:46:16.020
and then wildcard
seachable suffixes.

00:46:16.020 --> 00:46:19.990
So you can use that to emulate
something like partitioning,

00:46:19.990 --> 00:46:22.590
while providing yourself
a lot of flexibility

00:46:22.590 --> 00:46:25.327
from a sort of partition
management standpoint.

00:46:25.327 --> 00:46:27.660
So I think that's the thing
that people can kind of look

00:46:27.660 --> 00:46:29.701
at if they're used to
coming from something where

00:46:29.701 --> 00:46:32.634
list partitioning was really
key to getting a little bit

00:46:32.634 --> 00:46:33.300
performance out.

00:46:33.300 --> 00:46:36.336
Or just even organizing
large amounts of data.

00:46:36.336 --> 00:46:37.710
A good example of
this, actually,

00:46:37.710 --> 00:46:40.680
is a lot of the things we've
done with the ground station

00:46:40.680 --> 00:46:42.930
observation data and the
weather public data sets.

00:46:42.930 --> 00:46:44.638
This is a great example
of like, oh, this

00:46:44.638 --> 00:46:48.900
is we got a ton of data
from 1929 to today, gosh,

00:46:48.900 --> 00:46:50.760
we really ought to
figure out a way

00:46:50.760 --> 00:46:54.222
to deal with that in a
somewhat partitioned fashion.

00:46:54.222 --> 00:46:56.430
So, Dan, do you want to talk
about the Nesting stuff.

00:46:56.430 --> 00:46:56.670
DANIEL MINTZ: Yeah.

00:46:56.670 --> 00:46:57.480
DAN MCCLARY:
Because I think it's

00:46:57.480 --> 00:46:59.355
interesting to watch
someone from the outside

00:46:59.355 --> 00:47:00.980
think about our
nested structures.

00:47:00.980 --> 00:47:01.896
DANIEL MINTZ: Totally.

00:47:01.896 --> 00:47:03.790
And I'll be honest,
this challenges me.

00:47:03.790 --> 00:47:07.590
I'm a deeply relational thinker.

00:47:07.590 --> 00:47:11.812
I want things to be up and
down, side to side, and just--

00:47:11.812 --> 00:47:13.020
DAN MCCLARY: Tables are cool.

00:47:13.020 --> 00:47:14.520
DANIEL MINTZ: Yeah,
tables are good.

00:47:14.520 --> 00:47:16.140
Tables are good.

00:47:16.140 --> 00:47:17.280
Tables are good.

00:47:17.280 --> 00:47:20.560
So let's use a really
simple example.

00:47:20.560 --> 00:47:24.030
I can print it on the
screen simple example.

00:47:24.030 --> 00:47:26.610
I've got sessions, or
orders, or some data

00:47:26.610 --> 00:47:28.050
that is conceptually nested.

00:47:28.050 --> 00:47:32.250
In the real world, there is a
nesting pattern going on here.

00:47:32.250 --> 00:47:36.280
And so normally, no
pun intended, normally,

00:47:36.280 --> 00:47:38.280
the question that you
would ask yourself is well

00:47:38.280 --> 00:47:40.380
should I normalize this data?

00:47:40.380 --> 00:47:43.950
Which will be more efficient
on the space front,

00:47:43.950 --> 00:47:46.740
and the answer, as that
graph from before showed us,

00:47:46.740 --> 00:47:47.820
is don't bother.

00:47:47.820 --> 00:47:49.210
That's not a good reason.

00:47:49.210 --> 00:47:50.668
There may be good
reasons to do it,

00:47:50.668 --> 00:47:52.410
but space efficiency
is not a reason

00:47:52.410 --> 00:47:56.690
to do it when you have an
infinitely scalable cloud.

00:47:56.690 --> 00:47:58.890
OK so should I
denormalize it so I don't

00:47:58.890 --> 00:48:01.920
have to worry about joins.

00:48:01.920 --> 00:48:05.100
OK, normally the answer
would be, well, maybe.

00:48:05.100 --> 00:48:07.830
That might be more performant
for certain types of queries,

00:48:07.830 --> 00:48:09.960
but maybe not for others.

00:48:09.960 --> 00:48:12.777
And, in BigQuery at least,
there is another option.

00:48:12.777 --> 00:48:14.860
There's a third option,
which we never had before.

00:48:14.860 --> 00:48:18.260
So here is my really
simple example.

00:48:18.260 --> 00:48:20.520
I have three orders which
happened in January.

00:48:22.857 --> 00:48:24.690
This was very hard to
come up with because I

00:48:24.690 --> 00:48:28.620
had to figure out fruits and
vegetables that started with e.

00:48:28.620 --> 00:48:30.550
But I did it.

00:48:30.550 --> 00:48:32.760
And so we've got three orders.

00:48:32.760 --> 00:48:34.860
They each contain
some number of items.

00:48:34.860 --> 00:48:36.540
Each of those items
as a unit price.

00:48:36.540 --> 00:48:38.130
Super, super simple.

00:48:38.130 --> 00:48:42.000
So one thing that we
could do with this data

00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:44.820
is we could turn it
into a snowflake.

00:48:44.820 --> 00:48:49.050
We could have our orders
table, our orders items table,

00:48:49.050 --> 00:48:51.510
order items table,
and our items table.

00:48:51.510 --> 00:48:52.140
Great.

00:48:52.140 --> 00:48:52.910
Normalized.

00:48:52.910 --> 00:48:53.550
Yay!

00:48:53.550 --> 00:48:55.584
I feel so at peace,
because it's like--

00:48:55.584 --> 00:48:58.000
DAN MCCLARY: I'm pretty sure
did that in my first database

00:48:58.000 --> 00:48:59.450
class.

00:48:59.450 --> 00:49:03.030
I still hearken
back to those days.

00:49:03.030 --> 00:49:05.857
DANIEL MINTZ: I realized
that I organized my CDs.

00:49:05.857 --> 00:49:07.440
Some people may not
know what CDs are.

00:49:07.440 --> 00:49:11.850
They're these physical
disks, that we-- never mind.

00:49:11.850 --> 00:49:14.820
But I liked to do it in
order when I was a kid.

00:49:14.820 --> 00:49:19.420
Anyway, so we could do this,
and this would be totally fine.

00:49:19.420 --> 00:49:21.720
Another thing that we could
do is we could denormalize.

00:49:21.720 --> 00:49:24.270
We could stick it
all in one table.

00:49:24.270 --> 00:49:26.250
We've got some
repeated data here.

00:49:26.250 --> 00:49:30.770
We now know the unit
price of the banana twice.

00:49:30.770 --> 00:49:35.310
Unnecessary, space inefficient,
but, hey, no joins.

00:49:35.310 --> 00:49:37.200
Great.

00:49:37.200 --> 00:49:39.170
In BigQuery there
is a third option,

00:49:39.170 --> 00:49:42.370
which looks like this, which
no one scream from horror.

00:49:42.370 --> 00:49:46.750
This is really weird looking and
not OK for us who like tables,

00:49:46.750 --> 00:49:51.995
but we can just leave the
table nested inside a column.

00:49:53.902 --> 00:49:55.860
DAN MCCLARY: Daniel, we
heard you liked tables,

00:49:55.860 --> 00:49:58.830
so we put tables
inside your tables.

00:49:58.830 --> 00:50:02.620
DANIEL MINTZ: Is it turtles
all the way-- never mind.

00:50:02.620 --> 00:50:04.784
I'll give everybody a
second to compose themselves

00:50:04.784 --> 00:50:05.700
after looking at this.

00:50:05.700 --> 00:50:07.620
All right, so what
we have here is

00:50:07.620 --> 00:50:12.030
a table in BigQuery, which
still has an order ID,

00:50:12.030 --> 00:50:15.360
still has all the top level
information about an order.

00:50:15.360 --> 00:50:18.470
Order ID, order
date, order total.

00:50:18.470 --> 00:50:22.760
And then inside the order items
column, which is a column,

00:50:22.760 --> 00:50:28.500
we have, fundamentally,
another table in each row.

00:50:28.500 --> 00:50:31.050
They all have the same schema.

00:50:31.050 --> 00:50:34.050
And I'm not going to go into
the struts of arrays of struts

00:50:34.050 --> 00:50:36.400
of arrays of struts, but--

00:50:36.400 --> 00:50:39.810
DAN MCCLARY: That's a thing
we could talk about in Q&amp;A.

00:50:39.810 --> 00:50:42.630
DANIEL MINTZ: But this
is totally kosher,

00:50:42.630 --> 00:50:43.930
legit, in BigQuery.

00:50:43.930 --> 00:50:45.120
You can do this.

00:50:45.120 --> 00:50:48.120
Now, why would you
want to do this?

00:50:48.120 --> 00:50:53.010
Other than to drive your
Databases 101 teacher crazy.

00:50:53.010 --> 00:50:55.110
Well, let me go back.

00:50:55.110 --> 00:50:57.660
The reason is
actually because if I

00:50:57.660 --> 00:51:00.090
want to query something
just about orders,

00:51:00.090 --> 00:51:03.090
remember that
BigQuery is columnar.

00:51:03.090 --> 00:51:05.730
So that crazy
thing on the right,

00:51:05.730 --> 00:51:07.200
don't need to worry about it.

00:51:07.200 --> 00:51:09.150
Stays in memory,
I never touch it.

00:51:09.150 --> 00:51:12.510
Right I can get a count
of orders really simply.

00:51:12.510 --> 00:51:14.590
Select count of all from orders.

00:51:14.590 --> 00:51:16.350
It's going to give
me the answer.

00:51:16.350 --> 00:51:17.580
That's great.

00:51:17.580 --> 00:51:20.370
Now, when I do want
some information

00:51:20.370 --> 00:51:26.880
about the items in the orders,
I can unnest that structure,

00:51:26.880 --> 00:51:29.940
go in and get the information,
and then, and only then,

00:51:29.940 --> 00:51:32.430
unpack it and look at it.

00:51:32.430 --> 00:51:37.080
In the other examples, I have
to choose one or the other

00:51:37.080 --> 00:51:38.157
up front.

00:51:38.157 --> 00:51:40.740
I have to either say I'm going
to normalize because that makes

00:51:40.740 --> 00:51:44.040
it really easy to query
stuff about orders,

00:51:44.040 --> 00:51:45.720
but kind of a pain
to query stuff

00:51:45.720 --> 00:51:47.610
about the things in the orders
because now I have to do two

00:51:47.610 --> 00:51:48.360
joins.

00:51:48.360 --> 00:51:51.060
Remember again, this is an
incredibly simple dataset,

00:51:51.060 --> 00:51:52.890
so if you're dealing
with real data,

00:51:52.890 --> 00:51:54.720
this would actually
be a pain, not just

00:51:54.720 --> 00:51:57.080
kind of a fun exercise.

00:51:57.080 --> 00:51:59.660
In the denormalized one,
sure, I can get stuff

00:51:59.660 --> 00:52:02.990
about the items and the
orders really easily,

00:52:02.990 --> 00:52:05.990
but just to find out
how many orders I had,

00:52:05.990 --> 00:52:10.024
I have to do select count
of distinct order ID.

00:52:10.024 --> 00:52:11.690
And so now and scanning
a bunch of stuff

00:52:11.690 --> 00:52:14.660
repeatedly that I don't need.

00:52:14.660 --> 00:52:16.730
In this, I put off
the choice about how

00:52:16.730 --> 00:52:19.820
I want to structure the
data, until query time.

00:52:19.820 --> 00:52:22.220
Which is great, because I
can choose the structure that

00:52:22.220 --> 00:52:26.130
makes sense for the type of
queries that I want to run,

00:52:26.130 --> 00:52:28.200
This is a big deal
when you're dealing

00:52:28.200 --> 00:52:30.990
with billions of rows of data.

00:52:30.990 --> 00:52:33.230
So the one problem,
remember, we said, well,

00:52:33.230 --> 00:52:38.360
in our list of things we want
out of our data language,

00:52:38.360 --> 00:52:39.950
Is that writing
the SQL for this is

00:52:39.950 --> 00:52:41.240
going to look a little funny.

00:52:41.240 --> 00:52:45.710
We're going to use this idea
of left join unnest, which

00:52:45.710 --> 00:52:48.350
is weird, and a
little weird, right?

00:52:48.350 --> 00:52:54.200
So, but with a data platform,
with a third wave data tool,

00:52:54.200 --> 00:52:55.770
we're not writing the SQL.

00:52:55.770 --> 00:52:58.550
So I grabbed some look
LookML, which is our markup

00:52:58.550 --> 00:53:01.160
language, that deals with this.

00:53:01.160 --> 00:53:05.990
It says, yeah, there's this
table at the top called orders,

00:53:05.990 --> 00:53:09.830
and it has this join
called order items,

00:53:09.830 --> 00:53:14.210
and here are the dimensions
inside the views, inside orders

00:53:14.210 --> 00:53:15.320
and order items.

00:53:15.320 --> 00:53:18.110
I can compose things
like item total

00:53:18.110 --> 00:53:21.560
by multiplying quantity
and item price.

00:53:21.560 --> 00:53:25.040
I can specify
multiple time frames.

00:53:25.040 --> 00:53:27.830
And this probably
looks a little bit

00:53:27.830 --> 00:53:30.590
weird to people who
haven't seen LookML before,

00:53:30.590 --> 00:53:33.860
but the fun part is I can
learn this real quickly

00:53:33.860 --> 00:53:35.180
because I already speak SQL.

00:53:35.180 --> 00:53:37.460
And this is
fundamentally just SQL.

00:53:37.460 --> 00:53:42.089
And then I can expose
this to my business users.

00:53:42.089 --> 00:53:43.630
And so all of a
sudden, they can then

00:53:43.630 --> 00:53:48.100
explore this data set freely
and leave it to the tool,

00:53:48.100 --> 00:53:51.730
to the data platform, to
write the appropriate SQL.

00:53:51.730 --> 00:53:53.590
They don't worry about
how orders and order

00:53:53.590 --> 00:53:54.820
items are related.

00:53:54.820 --> 00:53:57.910
Whether I did the normalization,
the denormalization,

00:53:57.910 --> 00:54:00.877
the nested structure,
tool worries about that.

00:54:00.877 --> 00:54:03.460
So that stuff only gets written
once, and now all of a sudden,

00:54:03.460 --> 00:54:05.590
they can say, well, I want
to know orders by dates,

00:54:05.590 --> 00:54:08.470
I want to know how many bananas
were bought on the 26th.

00:54:08.470 --> 00:54:10.960
They can just freely move
around in this environment,

00:54:10.960 --> 00:54:12.130
and ask questions.

00:54:12.130 --> 00:54:15.840
We send out those queries
that the platform writes off

00:54:15.840 --> 00:54:19.390
to a BigQuery, and assuming that
there are more than seven rows

00:54:19.390 --> 00:54:22.570
of data, BigQuery, then,
can very powerfully

00:54:22.570 --> 00:54:24.787
churn through that data
and return an answer.

00:54:24.787 --> 00:54:26.620
DAN MCCLARY: Well you
preserve, importantly,

00:54:26.620 --> 00:54:29.515
you preserve the relational
model of the world throughout,

00:54:29.515 --> 00:54:30.640
DANIEL MINTZ: That's right.

00:54:30.640 --> 00:54:34.049
And so the relational
model now spans all the way

00:54:34.049 --> 00:54:36.340
from the bottom, from the
way that the data is actually

00:54:36.340 --> 00:54:39.970
structured in memory, all
the way to the top to the way

00:54:39.970 --> 00:54:42.010
that business people
are accessing the data.

00:54:42.010 --> 00:54:44.980
And so we have this
sort of unified model

00:54:44.980 --> 00:54:48.250
that is both how the
information is organized,

00:54:48.250 --> 00:54:51.052
at the most basic level, and
how people are accessing it.

00:54:51.052 --> 00:54:52.510
DAN MCCLARY: So at
least logically,

00:54:52.510 --> 00:54:54.680
we're actually accomplishing a
lot of what Codd set out to do.

00:54:54.680 --> 00:54:56.264
DANIEL MINTZ: Codd,
would be so happy!

00:54:56.264 --> 00:54:58.013
DAN MCCLARY: He'd
probably be freaked out.

00:54:58.013 --> 00:54:59.896
I think cell phones
would freak him out.

00:54:59.896 --> 00:55:04.990
DANIEL MINTZ: So I'm going to
hand it over to Dan to wrap up.

00:55:04.990 --> 00:55:08.620
If we're downstairs,
Looker's downstairs at E14,

00:55:08.620 --> 00:55:10.540
so as you're drinking
a beer, if you

00:55:10.540 --> 00:55:12.610
want to put our
engineers to the test

00:55:12.610 --> 00:55:16.180
and make them actually
explain LookML to you,

00:55:16.180 --> 00:55:18.730
on bigger data sets and
this, come on down to E14

00:55:18.730 --> 00:55:20.540
and you can make them do that.

00:55:20.540 --> 00:55:22.570
And I'll enjoy watching
them have to do it, so--

00:55:22.570 --> 00:55:24.130
DAN MCCLARY: Okay so I'm going
to try and wrap up really

00:55:24.130 --> 00:55:25.900
quickly so we maybe
have even one or two

00:55:25.900 --> 00:55:27.640
minutes for questions,
because I know there's also

00:55:27.640 --> 00:55:29.181
a happy hour that's
happening and I'm

00:55:29.181 --> 00:55:31.210
sure people are quite thirsty.

00:55:31.210 --> 00:55:33.730
I think for me though, the
core of this is really--

00:55:33.730 --> 00:55:37.090
the relational model matters,
normalization to some extent

00:55:37.090 --> 00:55:39.760
matters, it's at the foundation
of systems like BigQuery

00:55:39.760 --> 00:55:43.760
you don't get relational
query processors, SQL driven

00:55:43.760 --> 00:55:46.060
processors, without that model.

00:55:46.060 --> 00:55:48.190
And the logical
continuity of it, I think,

00:55:48.190 --> 00:55:50.470
is really important,
all the way from how

00:55:50.470 --> 00:55:52.210
we built our
distributed processing

00:55:52.210 --> 00:55:54.820
engine, to the way
you can model schema.

00:55:54.820 --> 00:55:57.970
Using not only the tools
you have learned, but also

00:55:57.970 --> 00:56:00.100
things like nested
and repeated fields.

00:56:00.100 --> 00:56:03.220
And it passes through tools like
Looker into the reporting space

00:56:03.220 --> 00:56:04.280
as well.

00:56:04.280 --> 00:56:06.130
But, I think, again,
it's important

00:56:06.130 --> 00:56:08.620
as we think about
trying tools BigQuery,

00:56:08.620 --> 00:56:11.890
trying tools like
Looker, we can't blindly

00:56:11.890 --> 00:56:13.205
accept the rules of the '70s.

00:56:13.205 --> 00:56:14.830
We have to know that
they're important,

00:56:14.830 --> 00:56:16.288
and know that they
have given birth

00:56:16.288 --> 00:56:19.990
to the point we're at now, but
anytime we're modeling data,

00:56:19.990 --> 00:56:22.900
we should just continue to ask
ourselves did this actually

00:56:22.900 --> 00:56:25.570
increase independence, data
independence, independence

00:56:25.570 --> 00:56:28.570
from our users, and their
ability to analyze data?

00:56:28.570 --> 00:56:30.760
Is it helping users
fundamentally do more,

00:56:30.760 --> 00:56:32.482
or find insights?

00:56:32.482 --> 00:56:33.940
Is it reducing the
inconsistencies?

00:56:33.940 --> 00:56:36.190
Is it making it easier for
me to actually figure out

00:56:36.190 --> 00:56:37.840
what happened?

00:56:37.840 --> 00:56:40.320
Is it making those outliers
come closer to the fore

00:56:40.320 --> 00:56:42.140
so I actually know
what's going on?

00:56:42.140 --> 00:56:45.070
And then finally, if we're
making a modeling choice,

00:56:45.070 --> 00:56:47.740
because we believe
performance is the reason,

00:56:47.740 --> 00:56:49.990
is it really meaningfully
enabling performance?

00:56:49.990 --> 00:56:51.980
Or is it really
kind of premature?

00:56:51.980 --> 00:56:54.160
Well you know it goes
a half a second faster,

00:56:54.160 --> 00:56:56.214
and that's really important.

00:56:56.214 --> 00:56:58.630
Is it really important, or is
it better to help the users?

00:56:58.630 --> 00:57:00.700
So again, all of the
things we learned

00:57:00.700 --> 00:57:05.110
from our friend the fish and
from Mr. Codd, are important.

00:57:05.110 --> 00:57:07.450
We just need to
make sure that we're

00:57:07.450 --> 00:57:09.910
aware that the systems
we work with now

00:57:09.910 --> 00:57:12.119
allow us to potentially
do quite a lot more.

00:57:12.119 --> 00:57:13.660
And with that we've
got only a couple

00:57:13.660 --> 00:57:15.826
of minutes left, but on
behalf of myself and Daniel,

00:57:15.826 --> 00:57:18.370
I'd really like to thank
you guys for sticking around

00:57:18.370 --> 00:57:19.960
even as happy hour started.

00:57:19.960 --> 00:57:21.160
We really appreciate it.

00:57:21.160 --> 00:57:23.530
Who at data modeling
could be so fun and fishy.

00:57:23.530 --> 00:57:25.030
[APPLAUSE]

00:57:25.030 --> 00:57:28.680
[MUSIC PLAYING]

